WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.845
Secure database connectivity should be testable.

2
00:00:02.845 --> 00:00:15.615
Tests can confirm that required configuration is present, malformed
secrets fail safely, production code does not accept development-only TLS
shortcuts, and environment-specific settings do not point to the wrong
database.

3
00:00:15.615 --> 00:00:20.709
Rotation readiness can be tested through controlled secret changes and
restart behavior.

4
00:00:20.709 --> 00:00:29.575
Dependency review should include database drivers, ORM versions, and
configuration defaults that affect authentication, TLS, pooling, or error
behavior.

5
00:00:29.575 --> 00:00:34.538
Logging should help operators understand connection health without
exposing secrets.

6
00:00:34.538 --> 00:00:48.366
Safe logs can record error categories, connection source, service
identity, environment, timing, correlation identifiers, and whether a
failure was authentication-related, network-related, certificate-related,
or permission-related.

7
00:00:48.366 --> 00:00:56.901
Unsafe logs include passwords, tokens, private keys, full sensitive DSNs,
certificate material, and large query payloads containing sensitive data.

8
00:00:56.901 --> 00:01:00.077
Redaction should be deliberate and tested, not assumed.

9
00:01:00.077 --> 00:01:03.319
Monitoring should make unusual database access visible.

10
00:01:03.319 --> 00:01:17.743
Useful signals include failed logins, unexpected client sources, new
accounts, privilege errors, disabled TLS where it is expected, unusual
connection counts, repeated connection failures, and application accounts
performing surprising operations.

11
00:01:17.743 --> 00:01:25.418
Database audit logs, application telemetry, secret-store access logs, and
platform logs all contribute different pieces of evidence.

12
00:01:25.418 --> 00:01:30.248
Monitoring is strongest when teams know which identities and access paths
are normal.

13
00:01:30.248 --> 00:01:35.078
Incident response planning turns connection security into recoverable
operations.

14
00:01:35.078 --> 00:01:51.089
If a credential, token, private key, or connection string is exposed,
teams should be able to identify affected systems, revoke or rotate
access, recycle pools, redeploy workloads, validate that old access no
longer works, review logs for suspicious activity, and prevent recurrence.

15
00:01:51.089 --> 00:01:56.250
The response should avoid panic changes that create outages or leave stale
sessions behind.

16
00:01:56.250 --> 00:02:01.080
Connection security must be observable without leaking the very secrets it
protects.
